 just  desperately wanted just to put my arms  around her and let her know that  we were still here and we still love to  desperately  um because i'm sure she she does she  feels abandoned she must do  and sometimes and there have been two  other instances that have come to  attention to us in the last few days a  police officer  who was visiting her mother in a care  home who stepped over the threshold to  hug her mother the patio doors are open  and her mother has been evicted from  that care home because the daughter  broke the rules now that daughter was a  police officer  and another lady who contacted us whose  daughter is is disabled she's in a care  home  her daughter has been restrained by two  care assistants because her daughter is  trying to hug her mother  and this has caused her mother to have a  mental breakdown she now has her own  crisis team  so this is affecting not only the people  in these care home settings but the  families too  so we are one of hundreds of thousands  of families in this position  and also my mother has been um  well she's been served an eviction  notice because i've actually stood my  ground  and refused to pay the fees so she had  she's actually due to leave the home  on the 21st of this month so she's had  an eviction notice i want to desperately  want to take her home and care for her  and literally we're having to jump  through all these  bureaucratic red hoops tape whatever you  want to call them  uh in order to to get her home and it's  just like  delay delay delay um even  though um the services are trying on our  behalf to get  my mother home it's there's still such a  quagmire  of um doubt and  misunderstanding i think and i think  what we have discovered as well  is we've known that the care system the  social care system hasn't really been  fit for purpose for a long time it  really relies so heavily on family  members to be there to care  and i think covid has been a stress test  to the system  and and we have to 